Every two positions contribute `5 * 4 = 20` choices. If the length is odd, index `0` leaves one extra even position, so it contributes another `5`.

That gives `20^(n / 2)`, with an extra factor of `5` when `n` is odd. The power is calculated by repeated squaring, so the large value of `n` is not a problem.

For example, when `n = 5`, there are two full pairs and one extra position:
`20^2 * 5 = 2000`.

```cpp
class Solution {
public:
    static const long long MOD = 1000000007LL;

    long long modPower(long long base, long long exponent) {
        long long result = 1;
        base %= MOD;

        while (exponent > 0) {
            if (exponent % 2 == 1) {
                result = (result * base) % MOD;
            }
            base = (base * base) % MOD;
            exponent /= 2;
        }

        return result;
    }

    int countGoodNumbers(long long n) {
        long long answer = modPower(20, n / 2);

        if (n % 2 == 1) {
            answer = (answer * 5) % MOD;
        }

        return static_cast<int>(answer);
    }
};
```

Time complexity: `O(log n)`.
Space complexity: `O(1)`.

There are 5 choices for every even index and 4 choices for every odd index. Thus the answer is:

`5^((n + 1) / 2) * 4^(n / 2)`

This version calculates each power by reading the exponent from the most significant bit to the least significant bit. At each step, the current result is squared, and the base is included when the bit is `1`.

The input length is not changed, and only a few `long long` variables are used.

```cpp
class Solution {
public:
    static const long long MOD = 1000000007LL;

    long long powerFromLeft(long long base, long long exponent) {
        long long result = 1;
        base %= MOD;

        if (exponent == 0) {
            return result;
        }

        long long highestBit = 1;
        while (highestBit <= exponent / 2) {
            highestBit *= 2;
        }

        while (highestBit > 0) {
            result = (result * result) % MOD;
            if ((exponent & highestBit) != 0) {
                result = (result * base) % MOD;
            }
            highestBit /= 2;
        }

        return result;
    }

    int countGoodNumbers(long long n) {
        long long evenCount = (n + 1) / 2;
        long long oddCount = n / 2;

        long long answer = powerFromLeft(5, evenCount);
        answer = (answer * powerFromLeft(4, oddCount)) % MOD;
        return static_cast<int>(answer);
    }
};
```

The zero-length exponent is handled directly, so a case with no odd indexes still works correctly.

Time complexity: `O(log n)`.
Space complexity: `O(1)`.

An even-indexed slot accepts 5 digits, while an odd-indexed slot accepts 4 digits. Counting the slots first avoids checking every possible number.

The helper uses the binary form of the exponent. When the current exponent bit is set, its base is multiplied into the answer. All products are reduced modulo `1_000_000_007`.

A quick check with `n = 2` gives `5 * 4 = 20`, which matches the two allowed positions.

```csharp
public class Solution {
    private const long MOD = 1_000_000_007L;

    private long ModPower(long baseValue, long exponent) {
        long result = 1;
        baseValue %= MOD;

        while (exponent > 0) {
            if ((exponent & 1) == 1) {
                result = (result * baseValue) % MOD;
            }

            baseValue = (baseValue * baseValue) % MOD;
            exponent >>= 1;
        }

        return result;
    }

    public int countGoodNumbers(long n) {
        long evenSlots = (n + 1) / 2;
        long oddSlots = n / 2;

        long answer = ModPower(5, evenSlots);
        answer = (answer * ModPower(4, oddSlots)) % MOD;

        return (int)answer;
    }
}
```

Time complexity: `O(log n)`.
Space complexity: `O(1)`.

At indexes `0, 2, 4, ...`, there are 5 allowed digits: `0, 2, 4, 6, 8`. At the other indexes, there are 4 prime digits: `2, 3, 5, 7`.

The number of even indexes is `(n + 1) / 2`, and the number of odd indexes is `n / 2`. The recursive helper uses exponentiation by squaring. It halves the exponent on each call instead of multiplying one position at a time.

The modulo is applied after every multiplication. This also keeps the values safe inside a Java `long`.

```java
class Solution {
    private static final long MOD = 1_000_000_007L;

    public int countGoodNumbers(long n) {
        long evenIndexes = (n + 1) / 2;
        long oddIndexes = n / 2;

        long evenWays = power(5, evenIndexes);
        long oddWays = power(4, oddIndexes);

        return (int) ((evenWays * oddWays) % MOD);
    }

    private long power(long base, long exponent) {
        if (exponent == 0) {
            return 1;
        }

        long half = power(base, exponent / 2);
        long result = (half * half) % MOD;

        if (exponent % 2 == 1) {
            result = (result * base) % MOD;
        }

        return result;
    }
}
```

For `n = 4`, the counts are 2 even indexes and 2 odd indexes, so the result is `5^2 * 4^2 = 400`.

Time complexity: `O(log n)`.
Space complexity: `O(log n)` because of the recursive calls.

